        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "#41346 looks correct.\r\n\r\nThe C-side fix of this PR is just being extra defensive, which Codex likes to do. It's assuming that `curwin` could've changed and/or become invalid before reaching \"Hand off to (async) vim.ui.select()\". Interestingly, this can actually happen because `parse_spelllang()` can trigger `SpellFileMissing` autocmd, where user can do such things. Impressive defense, but i don't think this is worth another fix on top of the already merged #41346.\r\n\r\nThe lua side fix of this PR is another extra-defensive measure against a weird implementation of vim.ui.select that uses another window and keeps focus on that window even after finishing selection. This too is an interesting edge case, but not worth another fix.\r\n\r\nClosing.\r\n\r\nAside: Now I'm wondering whether nvim should be more explicit about requirements on the user code that gets triggered inside nvim internals, so that AI coding tools can stop producing bloaty defense codes and instead blame user code. I probably wouldn't have even found those edge cases if i wrote the fix entirely myself, but Codex found them, and I thought, \"sure why not\".\r\n",
